WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.760
Breaking a carbon–carbon single bond takes,
on average, 347 kilojoules per mole.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:12.600
A double bond shares twice as many
electrons, so you might expect 694.

3
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.280
The average for a double bond is 612.

4
00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.420
Shorter and stronger, yes.

5
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.380
But not twice as strong.

6
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.320
So why isn't a double
bond twice as strong?

7
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.600
In this mission, you'll picture how
orbitals overlap to make a single bond,

8
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.800
and why it lets groups rotate freely.

9
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.900
Then you'll count the bonds in a molecule,

10
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.360
and see why some compounds
come in two forms.

11
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.400
Your mission: explain why a double bond
isn't twice as strong as a single bond.